---
/**
 * BAND. A full width surface.
 *
 * Two jobs, and the second one is the reason it exists rather than each
 * section rolling its own background.
 *
 * 1. It re-points the SEMANTIC tier for everything inside it. tokens.css says
 *    "re-theming happens by overriding this tier", so an inverted band sets
 *    --fg, --fg-secondary, --hairline-color and --bg to the on-inverted pair
 *    that already exists. Nothing nested has to know which surface it landed
 *    on. On Bucket B this reaches Tailwind too, because the colour half of
 *    tailwind-theme.css is deliberately NON inline, so text-fg inside an
 *    inverted band resolves to the inverted foreground at runtime.
 *
 * 2. It publishes data-surface, which is what SiteNav's surface script reads
 *    to decide the nav's own foreground. A band that forgets the attribute is
 *    invisible to the nav and the nav will keep the previous surface's ink.
 *    That is the exact failure that shipped on the old site, so the attribute
 *    is emitted by this component and is not optional.
 *
 * No literal value appears in this file.
 */
export type Surface = "canvas" | "inverted" | "accent";

interface Props {
  surface?: Surface;
  /** Rendered element. A band is usually a section, sometimes a div. */
  as?: "section" | "div" | "header" | "footer";
  class?: string;
  [key: string]: unknown;
}

/*
  REST IS SPREAD, AND IT HAS TO BE. Astro carries a parent's scoped-style
  marker (data-astro-cid-*) as a prop on the component invocation, so a
  component that destructures a fixed prop list and drops the rest silently
  loses it. The visible symptom is that a class passed from a parent lands on
  the element and none of the parent's rules match it. Found by measuring the
  rendered band, not by reading the markup: the probe bands came back 67px
  tall instead of 70vh.
*/
const { surface = "canvas", as: Tag = "section", class: className, ...rest } =
  Astro.props;
---

<Tag
  class:list={["band", `band--${surface}`, className]}
  data-surface={surface}
  {...rest}
>
  <slot />
</Tag>

<style>
  .band {
    background-color: var(--bg);
    color: var(--fg);
  }

  .band--inverted {
    --bg: var(--surface-inverted);
    --fg: var(--fg-on-inverted);
    /* The band has no separate secondary and muted. Both point at the same
       on-inverted token, because inventing a sixth grey is a ruling
       violation and the ramp does not carry an on-band secondary. */
    --fg-secondary: var(--fg-on-inverted);
    --fg-muted: var(--fg-muted-on-inverted);
    --hairline-color: var(--hairline-on-inverted);
    --ghost-color: var(--ghost-on-inverted);
  }

  .band--accent {
    --bg: var(--surface-accent);
    --fg: var(--fg-on-accent);
    --fg-secondary: var(--fg-on-accent);
    --fg-muted: var(--fg-muted-on-accent);
    --hairline-color: var(--hairline-on-accent);
    --ghost-color: var(--ghost-on-accent);
  }

  /*
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    MARKER TOKENS FOLLOW THE SURFACE. ADDED 2026-08-10.

    tokens.css sets --marker-hold and --marker-stroke per THEME, and a band
    inverts the ground WITHIN a theme, so the two disagreed and a marker on a
    band was drawn in the page's ink on the band's ground. Invisible in both
    directions, which is why reading the code did not catch it: in light,
    --marker-hold is --c-ink on a --c-band ground; in dark it is --c-band-fg on
    a --c-canvas ground. Dark on dark, then light on light.

    Nothing had shipped it yet only because the two showcases each carry a local
    copy of these two lines, which is the workaround, not the fix. The estate
    map is the first component to put markers straight onto a Band, and it
    should not have to know that.

    --fg is already the correct on-surface ink two rules up, so pointing the
    markers at it means a marker cannot disagree with the text beside it. This
    defines no colour: both sides are semantic tokens that already exist.
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  */
  .band--inverted,
  .band--accent {
    --marker-hold: var(--fg);
    --marker-stroke: var(--fg);
  }

  /*
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
    THE TAILWIND HALF USED TO LIVE HERE AND IT IS GONE. Removed 2026-08-11.

    This block re-declared `--color-fg` and three siblings at the band's own
    scope, because `@theme` emitted them at `:root` and a custom property is
    substituted where it is DECLARED, so re-pointing `--fg` on a descendant
    moved the semantic tier and left the Tailwind tier behind. Measured inside
    an inverted band at the time: `--fg` was #F5F5F5 and `--color-fg` was still
    #1a1916, so a `text-fg` paragraph rendered dark ink on the dark band, and on
    the accent band it rendered #1a1916 on #5E28C4, which is the nav defect
    reappearing on ordinary body copy.

    tailwind-theme.css now uses `@theme inline` throughout, which is the real
    fix and which that file documents with the measurements. Inline does not
    emit `--color-*` at all; it substitutes the reference straight into the
    utility, so `text-fg` resolves `var(--fg)` ON THE ELEMENT and a scoped
    override works without any help from here.

    That makes the four lines dead: they declared variables nothing reads.
    Verified before removal, no authored CSS on either site reads `var(--color-
    *)`, and tailwind-theme.css says outright not to. Dead code in the one file
    every surface passes through is worse than absent, because the next agent
    reads it as load bearing and copies the pattern.

    The comment above them was worse than the code. It asserted that the colour
    half of tailwind-theme.css is "deliberately NON inline", which is the exact
    opposite of what that file now does. This project has already lost a day to
    a comment about `@theme inline` that was backwards.
    ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  */
</style>
